counterexample:
-- No INSERT statements can create a counterexample as the queries are equivalent. However, to illustrate a hypothetical scenario where queries might differ, consider inserting data with Age values at the boundaries if one query were exclusive. For example:
INSERT INTO USERS (ID, DISPLAYNAME, AGE) VALUES (1, 'Alice', 19);
INSERT INTO USERS (ID, DISPLAYNAME, AGE) VALUES (2, 'Bob', 65);
INSERT INTO USERS (ID, DISPLAYNAME, AGE) VALUES (3, 'Charlie', 18);
INSERT INTO USERS (ID, DISPLAYNAME, AGE) VALUES (4, 'Diana', 66);
-- If Q1 were BETWEEN 19 AND 65 (inclusive) and Q2 were Age > 19 AND Age < 65 (exclusive), then Q1 would return Alice and Bob, while Q2 would return neither. But since Q1 and Q2 are both inclusive, both return Alice and Bob.

