counterexample:
-- No INSERT statements are needed as the queries are equivalent and will return the same results for any data. To illustrate a potential difference, if Q2 had WHERE T2.GPT <= 60 instead of < 60, the following data would cause different outputs:
-- INSERT INTO PATIENT (ID, BIRTHDAY) VALUES (1, '1990-01-01');
-- INSERT INTO LABORATORY (ID, DATE, GPT) VALUES (1, '2023-01-01', 60);
-- With this data, Q1 returns no rows (GPT=60 is not <60), while a modified Q2 with <= would return a row. However, based on the given queries, they are identical.

